{value: 0.5}\n  - ambient_temperature_range: {min:-25, max:55} °C\n  - validated software versions: strings \"3.27.xx ... 4.33.12 (types EXL, DIV-2, SMP)\". Summary.\n\nComponent 6: Calculating/indicating device (Liquid Controls)\n- role: \"Calculating/indicating device\"\n- manufacturer: Liquid Controls LLC\n- type_designations: [\"MASTERLOAD.iQ\", \"MASTERLOADx.iQ\"]\n- characteristics:\n  - environmental_class: \"M3 / E2 + E3\"\n  - accuracy_class: 0.5\n  - ambient_temperature_range: {min:-40, max:70} °C\n\nComponent 7: Gas elimination device\n- role: \"Gas elimination device\"\n- manufacturer: S.A.M.P.I. S.p.A., Liquid Controls LLC, Liquid Controls Europe\n- type_designations: [\"ESG_1\", \"ESG_2\"]\n- characteristics:\n  - minimum_pressure: {value: 0.1, unit_symbol: \"bar(g)\"} — unit \"bar(g)\"; unit_id null.\n  - maximum_pressure: conditional list:\n    - {\"condition\": \"with F and FD filters\", \"value\": {\"min\": 10, \"max\": 25}, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar(g)\"}\n    - {\"condition\": \"with FMP filters\", \"value\": {\"min\": 20, \"max\": 51}, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar(g)\"}\n    - {\"condition\": \"equal to the maximum p(e)max of the measuring system in which the Special Gas Extractor is used\", \"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 51}, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar(g)\"}\n  - environmental_class: \"M3 / E3\"\n  - ambient_temperature_range: {min:-25, max:55} °C\n  - product_temperature_range: {min:-10, max:50} °C\n  - intended_measurement: \"Hydrocarbon based oils and fuels with maximum viscosity of 20 mPa·s at 20 °C\"\n\nWait — the max pressure OCR: \"- 10; 25 with F and FD filters. - 20; 51 with FMP filters.\" Hmm, possibly \"10 – 25\" and \"20 – 51\"? Or \"−10; 25\" meaning from 10 to 25? I'll go with ranges {10,25} and {20,51}. Reasonable.\n\nAlso system-level components: \"Each measuring instrument consists at least of: One pump, One measurement transducer (meter), One calculating/indicating device (calculator), Optional one gas elimination device (gas extractor).\" The pump is not further specified. I could include a component for the pump: role \"Pump\", type_designations [] — no data. I'll mention in description instead.\n\nCertified type description: \"Interruptible measuring system for liquids other than water, type: (Un)Loading, transferring, measuring system for depot, oil terminal, airport. Consists at least of one pump, one measurement transducer (meter), one calculating/indicating device (calculator), optionally one gas elimination device (gas extractor).\"\n\nCertificate characteristics type_level (Table 1):\n- accuracy_class: values 0.3/0.5/1.0 — multi. Not a model matrix, just set of classes. Represent as string \"0.3 / 0.5 / 1.0\" or list? Structured value must be number/range/string. Use string. Hmm, but recommendation.accuracy_classes already captures [0.3, 0.5, 1.0]. For type_level accuracy_class I'll use string \"0.3 / 0.5 / 1.0\" with footnote \"*\"? The asterisk footnote: \"*) The actual characteristics are depending on the configuration and applicable parts chosen for the complete measuring system.\" Yes — footnote marker on accuracy class and intended measurement. I'll add footnotes entry: marker \"*)\", text \"The actual characteristics are depending on the configuration and applicable parts chosen for the complete measuring system.\"\n\n- intended_measurement: \"Liquid other than water with viscosity in the range 0.3 – 1242 mPa·s (excluding food liquids)\". Could split viscosity into a characteristic: viscosity_range: {min: 0.3, max: 1242} \"mPa·s\". Nice. And intended_measurement string with footnote.\n\nType-level characteristics:\n- accuracy_class: {\"value\": \"0.3 / 0.5 / 1.0\", \"footnote_markers\": [\"*)\"]}\n- viscosity_range: {\"value\": {\"min\": 0.3, \"max\": 1242}, \"unit_symbol\": \"mPa·s\"} — from \"viscosity in the range 0,3 - 1242 mPa·s\". Good.\n- intended_for_measurement_of: string with footnote.\n\nmodel_level for the system? Not needed. But wait — maybe I should add cert-level model_level entries for the sensor/meter to satisfy \"matrix detection\"? The rules emphasize matrix detection for cert-level families. Here matrices are component-level. I'll rely on matrix_tables and component model tables. I think that's the right call; matrix_tables explicitly \"preserve multi-dim tables\".\n\nShould models like CMF010 be in cert-level model_family? I'll not include cert-level model_family since the certified system type itself has no enumerated model IDs. Hmm... but the grader might expect model_level for the big Table 6. The example R137 had cert-level table → models + matrix_tables + model_level. Here Table 6 belongs to a component. The schema doesn't define component model_level. I could put component model matrices under components[].characteristics? No.\n\nDecision: matrix_tables for Tables 3, 4, 6 (+ maybe 2? Table 2 is simple key-value → component characteristics already). Also, to be safe, I can ALSO add cert-level model_level entries with attributes prefixed to indicate component, e.g. attribute \"measurement_transducer_cyclic_volume\"? That's inventing naming. I'll skip; matrix_tables suffice and are explicitly for that.\n\nHmm, actually let me reconsider once more. The instruction rule 4 is \"MOST IMPORTANT\" and emphasizes extracting model matrices into model_family/models and model_level. The R137 example is a cert where the whole instrument family is tabulated. Here, the certified type is a measuring system; its parts have model matrices. The best faithful representation: the matrix_tables contain the tables, and I can ALSO populate model_level scoped by component. But that duplicates. The schema says matrix_tables are for tables \"that don't fit elsewhere\" — Table 6 doesn't fit in model_level easily because it's component-scoped (different attribute space per component). So matrix_tables is the \"elsewhere\". I'm comfortable.\n\nAlternatively I could create model_level entries with component-scoped attributes like \"sensor_qmax\" — no.\n\nOK now test_reports. Dates format: keep as given \"2022-04-07\"? Or original text \"7 April 2022\"? I'll use ISO-like \"2022-04-07\" for machine parseability. Hmm, schema says date — either fine. I'll use ISO dates. date_issued: \"2022-04-07\".\n\nrevision_history: [{revision: \"0\", date: \"2022-04-07\", changes: \"Initial\"}] — also note \"This revision replaces the previous version.\" Include changes \"Initial (this revision replaces the previous version)\". Keep simple: \"Initial\".\n\nFootnotes:\n- {\"marker\": \"*\", \"text\": \"The actual characteristics are depending on the configuration and applicable parts chosen for the complete measuring system.\"} — the doc uses \"*)\". I'll use \"*\".\n- For Table 6: markers \"1)\"–\"6)\" and \"y)\". Add them.\n- Transmitter footnote \"[1]\": \"The transmitter software and the Weights & Measures (W&M) software form a matched set. Please note that the W&M software does not have a checksum and means W&M is licensed.\"\n- Transmitter \"*\": \"Between brackets the transmitter software is mentioned which belongs to the stated checksum.\"\n- 700 core \"*\": \"Indicated as 14AD, corrected from v3.2\"; \"**\": \"Linearization feature\".\n- 800 \"*\": \"Density Based Correction coefficients removed. To be used in gas application measuring Hydrogen or Helium.\"\n\nI'll include the key ones: \"*)\", \"y)\", \"1)\"...\"6)\", \"[1]\". Maybe also core processor ones. Keep the main ones to avoid bloat: I'll include \"*)\", \"y)\", \"1)\", \"2)\", \"3)\", \"4)\", \"5)\", \"6)\", \"[1]\", and maybe \"A)\", \"B)\", \"C)\" for CMF400 serial ranges as footnotes? Those are note items; I'll put them in the matrix table description.\n\nNow certificate:\n- number: \"R 117/2007-A-NL1-22.02\"\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: \"2638810\"\n- page_total: 15\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- date_issued: \"2022-04-07\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: \"NMi Certin B.V.\"\n- address_lines: [\"Thijsseweg 11