102 (3); 206 (5)\"}\n28. {\"type\": \"CMFHC4\", 0.5, 1700, 30000, 1000, \"102 (3); 206 (5)\"}\n\nFootnotes for this table: y), 1)-(6), and the (A)/(B)/(C) serial note. Include in \"footnotes\" field of the table and/or global footnotes. I'll add to the table's footnotes AND global footnotes (global for y)/1-6). Also description: \"Qmin and maximum pressure depend on the meter material code y); CMF400 variants (A)/(B)/(C) distinguished by serial number.\"\n\nAlso should I populate model_level for the sensor? Given rule 4's emphasis, maybe add cert-level model_level for sensor attributes with model ids like \"CMF100\"? But then attribute units kg/min etc. The models list would mix meters and sensors. I've decided: use matrix_tables. But hmm, let me reconsider adding model_family with the sensor models since Table 6 is the classic R137-style matrix... The rule says emit model_family.models and model_level when a 2-D table exists. The table here is for a component though. The components[] structure is the right home. I'll also add \"model_level\" under... no, components don't have that slot. \n\nCompromise: I'll populate cert-level model_level entries scoped with component prefix in the attribute? No. Final: matrix_tables only. I accept the tradeoff.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider — maybe I should include a cert-level model_family for the measurement sensor models, since it's the dominant matrix? But the certificate's certified type is the system; declaring models CMF010... as system models would misrepresent. I'll stick with matrix_tables + components.\n\nAlso Table 2 (measurement transducer general characteristics) → component characteristics (done). Table 5 (sensor general) → component characteristics (done). Tables 7-11 → component characteristics (done).\n\nNow certificate-level type_level characteristics (from Table 1):\ncharacteristics:\n  type_level:\n    accuracy_class: {value: \"0.3 / 0.5 / 1.0\", footnote_markers: [\"*)\"]}\n    viscosity_range: {value: {min: 0.3, max: 1242}, unit_symbol: \"mPa·s\"}\n    intended_measurement: {value: \"Liquid other than water (excluding food liquids)\", footnote_markers: [\"*)\"]}\n\nHmm \"Intended for the measurement of: Liquid other than water with viscosity in the range 0,3 - 1242 mPa·s (excluding food liquids) *\". So intended_measurement full string + separate viscosity_range numeric. Good.\n\nmodel_level: [] — omit? Schema shows model_level[] — can omit if empty. config_level: maybe the configuration notes? Not really numeric. Omit.\n\nmatrix_tables: 3 tables.\n\nWait, also should Table 6 type designations include material suffix y codes? Fine as is.\n\nComponent list — also the \"dual pulse converter\" mentioned in env classes for core processor (\"M3 / E3 for 700, 800, 820, 1700, 2700, 3500, 3700 and dual pulse converter\"). That's a sub-thing; keep in the environmental_class string.\n\nAlso the measurement transducer component includes the Eltomatic impulse encoder — could be a components[] entry with role \"Impulse encoder (pulser)\", type \"Eltomatic A/S Impulse Encoder, type 01-09\", alternatives null, part of measurement transducer. I'll add it as a component (role: \"Impulse encoder (of the measurement transducer)\"). Good per rule 6 (sub-assemblies).\n\nAlso mention the Eltomatic impulse encoder: yes as component.\n\nComponents array final:\n\n1. role: \"Measurement transducer (meter)\"\n   manufacturer: \"Silea Liquid Transfer S.r.l.\"\n   type_designations: [\"DM\", \"BMxxx\"]\n   documentation_folder: \"TC12118-1\"\n   characteristics: {...}\n   \n   Hmm wait — do I add extra keys manufacturer/documentation_folder to component objects? I decided yes. It aids extraction. Slight schema deviation but additive.\n\n2. role: \"Impulse encoder / pulser (part of measurement transducer)\"\n   manufacturer: \"Eltomatic A/S\"\n   type_designations: [\"01-09\"]\n\n3. role: \"Measurement sensor\"\n   manufacturer: \"Emerson Process Management Flow B.V.\"\n   type_designations: [\"CMFxxxy\", \"DS600\"] — page 4 header says \"DMFxxxy, DS600\" but Table 5/6 say CMFxxxy; page 2 also CMFxxxy. Use CMFxxxy. Maybe include both? Use \"CMFxxxy\" (and note DS600). \n   documentation_folder: \"TC7056-13\"\n   characteristics: as above.\n\n4. role: \"Core processor\"\n   manufacturer: \"Emerson Process Management Flow B.V.\"\n   type_designations: [\"700\", \"800\", \"820\", \"1700\", \"2500\", \"2700\", \"3500\", \"3700\"]\n   characteristics:\n     series: \"MVD series\"\n     environmental_class: \"M3 / E3 (for 700, 800, 820, 1700, 2700, 3500, 3700 and dual pulse converter); M2 / E2 (for 2500)\"\n     ambient_temperature_range: -40/+55 °C\n     validated_software: \"Versions and checksums validated per processor family: 700 (2.0–3.52), 800 (3.11–5.30), 820 (1.00–1.50), 1700/2700 (5.0/1.0–8.02/1.3; versions 3.2–4.2 without checksums), 2500 (4.0/1.x–8.02/1.3; versions 4.0–4.2 without checksums), 3500/3700 (7.0/1.1–8.51-ETO22243)\"\n   documentation_folder: \"TC7057-17\"\n\n5. role: \"Flow transmitter\"\n   manufacturer: \"Emerson Process Management Flow B.V.\"\n   type_designations: [\"5700*1(2,3 or 5)A***ZZ*\"]\n   documentation_folder: \"TC8519-4\"\n   characteristics:\n     environmental_class: \"M3 / E3 / H3\"\n     accuracy_class: \"0.3 / 0.5 / 1.0 / 1.5 / 2.5\"\n     ambient_temperature_range: [list of two conditionals]\n     validated_software: \"Transmitter software (Weights & Measures) 1.20 (1.0)–4.1 (4.0); internal core software 4.02–5.22; PIC firmware 8.0; LCD PIC firmware 3.0\"\n\n6. role: \"Calculating/indicating device\"\n   manufacturer: \"Toptech Systems, Inc.\"\n   type_designations: [\"MultiLoad II\"]\n   documentation_folder: \"TC7311-7\"\n   characteristics:\n     environmental_class: \"M3 / E2\"\n     accuracy_class: 0.5\n     ambient_temperature_range: -25/+55\n     validated_software: \"Older versions without checksums: 3.27.xx–3.31.xx (xx ≤ 23), 3.28.xx–3.30.xx, 4.30.xx, 4.31.xx (xx ≤ 23); versions with checksums for types EXL and DIV-2: 3.31.24–4.33.12; for type SMP: 3.31.24–4.33.12\"\n\n7. role: \"Calculating/indicating device\"\n   manufacturer: \"Liquid Controls LLC\"\n   type_designations: [\"MASTERLOAD.iQ\", \"MASTERLOADx.iQ\"]\n   documentation_folder: \"TC11730-1\"\n   characteristics:\n     environmental_class: \"M3 / E2 + E3\"\n     accuracy_class: 0.5\n     ambient_temperature_range: -40/+70\n     validated_software: \"GNU/Linux 4.9.88-007; LCR.iQ SR1000 v1.07–v1.11.00; I/O board SR1010 v1.06–v1.11.00; SENSEiQ SR1011 v1.00.01–v1.11.00\"\n\n8. role: \"Gas elimination device (optional)\"\n   manufacturer: \"S.A.M.P.I. S.p.A.; Liquid Controls LLC; Liquid Controls Europe\"\n   type_designations: [\"ESG_1\", \"ESG_2\"]\n   documentation_folder: \"TC7329-5\"\n   characteristics:\n     minimum_pressure: {value: 0.1, unit_symbol: \"bar(g)\"}\n     maximum_pressure: [conditional list]\n     environmental_class: \"M3 / E3\"\n     ambient_temperature_range: -25/+55\n     product_temperature_range: -10/+50\n     intended_measurement: \"Hydrocarbon based oils and fuels with maximum viscosity of 20 mPa·s at 20 °C\"\n\nComponent characteristics structured values: each is {\"value\": ..., \"unit_symbol\": ..., \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []}? Rule 2 says every characteristic value MUST be an object with that shape. unit_id — \"Leave unit_id: null unless certain\". Should I include unit_id key? The shape includes it; I'll include unit_id: null everywhere or omit? \"Leave unit_id: null unless you are certain\" → include as null. I'll include unit_id: null on numeric/unit values. For pure strings maybe omit unit_symbol? Shape says unit_symbol can be null. To be consistent I'll include unit_symbol and unit_id keys on all characteristic value objects. footnote_markers only when relevant (or empty array). I'll include empty arrays? Rule 8 says omit what's absent — but the shape is mandated. I'll include footnote_markers only when non-empty to reduce noise. Hmm, strictness: \"Every characteristic value MUST be an object with this shape\". I'll include all four keys: value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers. That's verbose but compliant. Actually for strings like environmental class, unit_symbol null, unit_id null, footnote_markers []. Fine.\n\nFor conditional lists (ambient temp of transmitter, max pressure of gas extractor), shape deviates — list of {condition, value, unit_symbol, footnote_markers}. Acceptable per rule 5 pattern.\n\nFor the certificate-level characteristics.type_level — same structured shape.\n\nNow regarding the \"pump\" — component? No type given. Mention in description only. OK.\n\nRevision history: revision \"0\" (number says revision 0), date 2022-04-07, changes \"Initial\". The table says Revision \"Initial\", Date 7 April 2022. So revision field: the history table's revision column value is \"Initial\". Schema: revision (string, e.g. \"0\"). I'll set revision: \"0\", date: \"2022-04-07\", changes: \"Initial\". Good.\n\ncertificate.revision? The number line: \"Number R 117/2007-A-NL1-22.02 revision 0\". The schema has revision_history but not a cert revision field. Fine.\n\nFootnotes global:\n- {\"marker\": \"*)\", \"text\": \"The actual characteristics are depending on the configuration and applicable parts chosen for the complete measuring system.\"}\n- {\"marker\": \"y)\", \"text\": \"Indicates the type of material the meter is built of (see material codes 1)–6)).\"}\n- {\"marker\": \"1)\", \"text\": \"y = H, L or M\"}\n- {\"marker\": \"2)\", \"text\": \"y = P (high pressure version)\"}\n- {\"marker\": \"3)\", \"text\": \"y = A, L or M\"}\n- {\"marker\": \"4)\", \"text\": \"y = B, C, E or H\"}\n- {\"marker\": \"5)\", \"text\": \"y = Y\"}\n- {\"marker\": \"6)\", \"text\": \"y = A, B, C or E\"}\n- {\"marker\": \"[1]\", \"text\": \"The transmitter software and the Weights & Measures (W&M) software form a matched set. The W&M software does not have a checksum; its presence means W&M is licensed.\"}\n- {\"marker\": \"*)\", ...} — conflict: \"*)\" used both for Table 1 asterisk and transmitter LCD note and 700 checksum note. Multiple different texts with same marker. I'll include the Table 1 one, and for transmitter: {\"marker\": \"*) (transmitter software table)\", ...}? Messy. I'll include the Table 1 footnote and the sensor table footnotes and [1]. For the checksum footnotes (700 \"*\": \"Indicated as 14AD, corrected from v3.2\"; \"**\": \"Linearization feature\"; 800 \"*\": \"Density Based Correction coefficients removed...\"), I could add with distinct markers like \"*) (700 core processor)\". Hmm, that's editorializing but keeps clarity. I'll include a few with qualified markers. Actually simpler: skip those three, or include as one footnote each with marker as-is. Duplicate markers with different texts would be confusing. I'll qualify: \"*) (700 core processor table)\", \"**) (700 core processor table)\", \"*) (800 core processor table)\", \"*) (5700 transmitter LCD PIC firmware table)\". I think that's helpful and faithful.\n\nCMF400 (A)(B)(C) — I'll put in the matrix table description rather than footnotes.\n\nNow footnote markers on Table 6 values: the parenthetical codes are within the value strings (e.g. \"125 (3); 225 (4); 413 (2)\"). That preserves association. Also add footnote_markers on those cells? The value string includes \"(3)\" etc. — I'll leave the codes inline and rely on footnotes list. Good enough.\n\nAlternatively make values numeric and footnote markers separate — but multiple conditional values per cell forces strings. Fine.\n\nAccuracy class in matrix rows: numeric 0.3/0.5. Good.\n\nNow the test_reports list — let me finalize entries with ISO dates:\n\ntype_evaluation_report:\n- {\"id\": \"NMi-2638810-01\", \"date\": \"2022-04-07\", \"pages\": 50, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n\ndocumentation_file:\n- {\"id\": \"T12216-1\", \"role\": \"documentation_file\"} (documentation folder of the measuring system)\n- TC12118-1 (measurement transducer)\n- TC7